 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, the worker evicts enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction
+
+The cache splits its entries into two queues so that a long sequential screening cannot displace the smaller working set a comparison session reuses:
+
+- **Probationary queue** — first-seen sheets, held in FIFO order. A screening that touches each sheet once fills and drains this queue without reaching the reused set.
+- **Protected queue** — sheets that have proven reuse, held in LRU order. The comparison sheets editors revisit live here.
+
+A bounded **eviction ghost list** records the identifiers (clip ID, revision, renderer version) — never the JPEG bytes — of the sheets most recently evicted from the probationary queue. It carries the reuse signal: a sheet counts as reused when it is requested again after its bytes have already been evicted once.
+
+**Admission.** When a render completes and its sheet is admitted:
+
+- If its key is on the ghost list, the request that triggered the render is a reuse; admit the sheet to the protected queue (most-recent end) and drop the key from the ghost list.
+- Otherwise admit it to the probationary queue (FIFO tail) as a first-seen sheet.
+
+**Cache hits.** A 200 served from the cache refreshes the protected queue's LRU order when the entry lives there. A hit on a probationary entry does not promote it — promotion happens only through the ghost list, after eviction and re-request. This is what makes a one-pass screening scan-resistant: sheets touched once stay in the probationary queue and age out in FIFO order rather than being mistaken for the reused working set.
+
+**Eviction.** When admitting a sheet would exceed 12 GiB, the worker evicts from the probationary queue first (FIFO head), recording each evicted key on the ghost list; the ghost list is itself bounded and drops its oldest identifier when full. Only when the probationary queue is empty does eviction fall to the protected queue's LRU end.
+
+A **probationary-share** parameter bounds how much of the 12 GiB budget the probationary queue may hold, reserving the remainder for the protected set. Its value is a tuning concern (see Open Questions); the design fixes the mechanism, not the numbers.
+
+The external interface, the four-job render queue, and the 12 GiB disk budget are unchanged by this policy.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q — a probationary queue and a protected queue with an eviction ghost list | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons, so a one-pass scan must not evict the reused set. 2Q confines a screening to the probationary queue, protecting the comparison sheets. LRU needs the least bookkeeping but a long scan evicts the working set; aged frequency counts retain popular clips but add counter maintenance and lag a project switch. The extra queues and the share parameter are the accepted cost of scan resistance. |
+| Reuse signal | Re-request after eviction, tracked by a ghost list of evicted identifiers | Promote on a second hit while the entry is still in the probationary queue | Promoting only on re-reference after eviction is what resists scans: a sheet touched repeatedly within one screening pass is not mistaken for a reused comparison sheet. The ghost list stores identifiers only, so the signal costs a bounded amount of metadata, not cached bytes. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy is 2Q (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. The probationary-share parameter value and the ghost list's bounded length — tuning concerns to set against observed pilot workloads once real screening and comparison traffic is available.
